  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: All models are depicting an upper level \nlow pressure system diving to the south from the Pacific Northwest \nstates to just off the coast of Western California early next week. \nWinds will be breezy to locally windy with gusts of 25 to 40 mph. \nHighs will be several degrees cooler with readings in the 50s and \n60s. Overnight lows will be in the 30s and 40s.\n\nA slow forward progression is in store for this storm system as it \ntries to move east across Northern and Central Nevada Wednesday into \nFriday. Valley rain showers and mountain snow showers are likely \neach day. The estimated three day forecast amounts for precipitation \nand snowfall are coming in around one-half to two-thirds of an inch \nof rainfall in the valleys with one to four inches of snow across \nthe highest elevations in the mountains. Afternoon highs both of \nthese days will be in the 50s and 60s, with overnight lows in the \n30s and 40s.\n\nThe system may be exiting east by the latter portions of the week. \nHighs will remain on the cool side, with readings in the 50s and 60s \nand lows in the 30s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: No changes were made to the\nshort term grids as NBM is handling the forecast well. Confidence\nin the forecast remains high through Monday. Confidence continues\nto be moderate with respect to the precipitation and temperature \nforecast next week. Low confidence on the chances of a cold front\nlate next week.",
